window.CS_NARR=window.CS_NARR||{};window.CS_NARR["ovrisk"]={"ovriskatlas": [[null, ["Hi, Buddy here. This sheet is about risk management for Indian markets, condensed from OpenAlgo's free Open Varsity course. It starts outside the market, with the money safety base, then moves through investor allocation, trader sizing and limits, leverage and execution, and finally F and O. If you only take one idea away, make it this: survive first. Every percentage and rupee figure here is the course's illustration, not a personal recommendation, and this is education, not investment advice."]], ["risk-management-topic-atlas", ["Here's why the sheet runs in this order. Before any rupee goes into the market, you need to know what risk really is and whether the money is ready, so basics and money safety come first. Investor risk follows, because most people start as investors. Trader risk adds sizing, stops and limits. Leverage and execution show how risk multiplies, and F and O risk comes last, because it combines everything before it."]], ["how-to-use-this-sheet", ["How to use this sheet. Run the \"is this money ready\" ladder before any rupee enters the market. If you're an investor, read money safety, then the investor section on allocation, rebalancing and concentration. If you're a trader, read position sizing, stop losses, limits and heat, then leverage and execution. And if you're new to F and O, read the F and O section only after the base and sizing are in place."]], ["is-this-money-ready-for-the-market", ["This ladder asks whether a sum of money is ready for the market. First: is an emergency fund of 3 to 6 months of expenses filled and untouched? If not, build the base first, in cash or a liquid fund you can reach in a day. If yes: do you have any credit card or personal loan balance outstanding? If yes, clear the costly debt first; an 18% loan repaid is a guaranteed 18%. If not: will you need this money within about 3 years? If yes, it belongs in a goal bucket and should stay safe, in a liquid fund, FD or short debt. If not: could you lose all of it without your life changing? If yes, it can go into a small trading or speculation slice, sized so a total loss is survivable. If not, it belongs in the long term investing bucket: diversified, with a horizon of 5 years or more.", "The takeaway: Emergency fund first, costly debt cleared, goal money kept safe; only money you could lose entirely goes to speculation.", "Pause and answer: You have a credit-card balance at 18%. What does the ladder say before investing?", "⏸", "Answer: Clear the costly debt first: repaying an 18% loan is a sure 18% return."]]], "basics": [[null, ["Section one: risk is not just losing money."]], ["what-risk-really-means", ["What risk really means. Risk is anything that can knock you out of the game: lost capital, but also lost sleep, lost discipline, lost time, and above all being forced to sell at the worst price. Separate a temporary dip, where a sound asset falls and can recover, from a permanent loss, where the business is broken, or a sound asset was sold at the bottom. Arjun put ₹3 lakh into large companies in January 2020. The Nifty fell about 38% by late March, his portfolio showed about ₹1.85 lakh, and he sold. The market made new highs within about a year. The sale, not the fall, made the loss permanent. Three things create forced sellers: leverage, where the broker squares you off; money needed soon; and emotional capacity running out. And the quiet, self made risks matter most: widening a stop \"just this once\", putting 40% of savings into one weekly expiry, or taking leverage on a calm day. Calm isn't safe. A simple test: would a 35% fall trigger a margin call or break this month's budget? Then the position is too big.", "The takeaway: Risk is anything that knocks you out of the game; a temporary dip only becomes permanent when you sell at the bottom.", "Pause and answer: What turned Arjun's 2020 dip into a permanent loss?", "⏸", "Answer: Selling near the bottom; the market made new highs within about a year."]], ["survive-first-the-recovery-math", ["Survive first: the recovery maths. The gain needed to get back is the loss divided by one minus the loss. A 10% loss needs 11% to recover. 25% needs 33%. 50% needs 100%. 75% needs 300%, and 90% needs 900%. Risk of ruin is the chance losses get so deep you can't continue, and it rises fast with the share of the account risked per trade. Risk about a third per trade and 3 or 4 losses in a row finish you: three such losses leave about 30%. Risk 1 to 2% and 10 straight losses still leave about 82 to 90% of the account. In COVID 2020 the Nifty fell about 38 to 40% in weeks and needed about 65% to recover, close to a year, and that was a diversified index. Ravi grew ₹1 lakh to about ₹1.3 lakh, then a gap down on an oversized leveraged position left ₹41,000 by lunch. The mistake was size, not direction. SEBI's September 2024 study found about 91% of individual equity derivatives traders lost money in FY24, and uncontrolled size is a central cause. After losses, trade smaller, never bigger.", "The takeaway: Losses compound against you: −50% needs +100% to recover, so risk small enough that a losing streak can't ruin you.", "Pause and answer: What gain recovers a 50% loss? A 25% loss?", "⏸", "Answer: +100% and about +33%."]], ["risk-vs-reward-name-the-risk", ["Risk versus reward: name the risk. Known risks are visible and measurable, like a 38 to 40% index crash; plan and size for them. Unknown risks are fraud, war, rule changes and pandemics; never bet so big that one surprise ends you. Ignored risks are seen but denied, like moving a stop or holding a mistake; stop pretending, because they're avoidable. You're paid only for carrying risk. An FD at about 7% is low risk and low reward, while a small cap multibagger can also fall 70 to 80% or become impossible to exit. And \"guaranteed 3% a month\" compounds to about 43% a year: a high return with \"no risk\" is a lie, often paid to early joiners from later money. Before any investment, ask four questions. What's the reward in rupees, and when? Where exactly is the risk? If nobody can say, that's the answer. What's the worst realistic loss, and could you survive it? And why is this being offered to me?", "The takeaway: You're paid only for carrying risk; size for known risks, never bet so big an unknown one ends you, and stop denying the ignored ones.", "Pause and answer: 'Guaranteed 3% a month, no risk': what's wrong with it?", "⏸", "Answer: It compounds to about 43% a year; a high return with no risk is a lie, often a scam."]], ["the-risk-pyramid", ["The risk pyramid. The base sits outside the market: an emergency fund of 3 to 6 months, health and term insurance, and no high interest debt. Above it is investing, for goals 5 or more years out, in index funds and quality businesses: the bulk of your money. Then trading, over days to weeks, with a planned exit and stop on every position: a thinner layer. And at the tip, speculation, like weekly F and O and hot small caps: only money you'd never miss. A teaching split, per ₹100 after the base, is ₹80 investing, ₹15 trading and ₹5 speculation, and many beginners keep the top two at zero while learning. Some money never belongs in the market: rent, this month's EMI, fees due in 1 to 3 years, and borrowed or margin funded money. Meera, 26, put a ₹1.2 lakh bonus into expiry day Bank Nifty options. By 3 pm it was about ₹18,000, and ₹40,000 of it was her father's surgery deposit. The error was letting money that wasn't hers to risk into the market at all.", "The takeaway: Build from the base up: safety, then investing, then a thinner trading layer, with speculation only from money you'd never miss.", "Pause and answer: What sits in the base, outside the market?", "⏸", "Answer: An emergency fund of 3–6 months, health and term insurance, and no high-interest debt."]], ["hidden-risk-by-participant", ["Hidden risk by participant. The long term investor chases compounding, but ignores the risk of overpaying for a great company, or believes \"it always comes back\"; the worst case is panic selling near the bottom of a 50 to 60% fall. The stock trader chases quick gains, but ignores losing streaks and costs and slippage eating the edge; the worst case is a gap through the stop. The F and O buyer chases a big payoff from a small outlay, but ignores time decay, and most buyers lose over time; the worst case is the premium going to zero. And the option seller chases steady income and a high win rate, but ignores the rare violent move; the worst case is one overnight gap erasing months of premium.", "The takeaway: Each participant chases a reward and ignores a matching risk; the option seller's is the rare violent move.", "Pause and answer: What risk does an option seller typically ignore?", "⏸", "Answer: The rare violent move: one overnight gap can erase months of premium."]]], "base": [[null, ["Section two: the money safety system, the base of the pyramid."]], ["fix-your-base-five-pillars", ["Fix your base with five pillars. An emergency fund of about 6 months of expenses, in cash or a liquid fund, never in stocks; at ₹40,000 a month, that's ₹2.4 lakh. Health insurance: your own policy for you and your family, not only the employer's. Term life: only if others depend on your income, and pure cover with no investment mixed in. No costly debt: cards and personal loans charge 14 to 40%, and clearing 18% debt is a sure, tax free 18%. And income stability: salaried people need about 6 months of buffer, freelancers and business owners 9 to 12. Arjun had ₹4 lakh invested and no buffer. He lost his job in early 2020 and sold near the March lows to pay rent, and the market roughly doubled off the lows within a year. The base turns a life shock into a personal problem instead of a forced sale.", "The takeaway: Emergency fund, your own health cover, term life if others depend on you, no costly debt, and a buffer matched to income stability.", "Pause and answer: How many months of buffer for a freelancer versus a salaried person?", "⏸", "Answer: 9–12 months for freelancers and business owners, about 6 for salaried."]], ["capital-buckets-and-the-walls-between-them", ["Capital buckets, and the walls between them. Long term investing, for 5 years or more, in index funds, SIPs and quality stocks: the largest bucket. Short term goals, 1 to 3 years out, like a car, a wedding or a deposit: kept whole, sized as needed. Trading, over days to weeks, always with a stop and a plan: small and capped. F and O and speculation, where you assume total loss is possible: tiny. And learning: the tuition you pay while learning, a few thousand rupees, the smallest. The rule is that a loss in one bucket must never drain another. Keep goal money in an FD or liquid fund you'd have to break consciously. An empty risk bucket means the system worked, so stop, and refill only from genuinely spare money. Arjun ran his salary, savings and weekly options through one account, and one bad expiry cost about ₹35,000, his sister's college fee due in two months.", "The takeaway: Separate buckets for long-term, goals, trading, speculation and learning; a loss in one must never drain another.", "Pause and answer: What's the one rule between buckets?", "⏸", "Answer: A loss in one bucket must never drain another."]], ["time-horizon-picks-the-asset", ["Time horizon picks the asset. Money needed in under a year sits in a savings account or liquid fund. 1 to 3 years: mostly debt funds and short FDs. 3 to 7 years: balanced debt and equity. And 7 years or more: mostly equity, index funds and quality stocks. For short money, the danger is that equities can fall 30 to 50% and stay down for years, so when the need date and the market clash, you sell low. For long money, the danger is inflation: prices rising about 6% a year against 3 to 4% in savings is a guaranteed slow loss. Arjun moved an ₹8 lakh house down payment into stocks in late 2019; it was about ₹5.2 lakh in March 2020 when the builder's payment fell due. The mismatch was the mistake. And shorter deadlines, like an F and O expiry, need tighter risk control, not looser, because you can't wait for recovery.", "The takeaway: Short money stays out of equity; long money must beat inflation, so it leans on equity.", "Pause and answer: Money needed in 1–3 years: where?", "⏸", "Answer: Mostly debt funds and short FDs."]], ["capacity-vs-tolerance", ["Capacity versus tolerance. Capacity is what your finances can survive: arithmetic. Tolerance is what your nerves can hold through a fall. Your usable risk is the lower of the two. A young salaried person has high capacity but untested tolerance, so moderate risk while building the habit. A retiree has low capacity but is often calm, so low risk, because capacity caps it. A business owner with lumpy income has unstable capacity, so low to moderate risk with a bigger base. And a full time trader has fragile capacity but high tolerance, so moderate risk with strict per trade caps. To set your risk budget: keep the emergency fund full and score your capacity from income, dependents, debt and horizon. Score your tolerance with the 2 am test: at minus 20%, would you hold, freeze, or sell? Take the lower score and turn it into rupees: ₹2 lakh at 20% is ₹40,000 of pre accepted pain. Then write it down, and re check yearly and after life changes. Tolerance is overstated in bull markets.", "The takeaway: Usable risk is the lower of what your finances can survive and what your nerves can hold.", "Pause and answer: Define capacity and tolerance.", "⏸", "Answer: Capacity is what your finances can survive; tolerance is what your nerves can hold through a fall."]]], "investor": [[null, ["Section three: investor risk management."]], ["asset-allocation-the-biggest-decision", ["Asset allocation is the biggest decision. The split across equity, debt, gold and cash sets how violent the ride is, while stock picking only decides how you do against the market. A 40% market fall costs a 70% equity portfolio about 28%, and a 30% equity portfolio about 12%. From January to March 2020, the Nifty fell about 38%, gold in rupees rose about 8%, and debt was about flat. A 60, 30, 10 mix lost about 22% and needed about 28% to recover, against about 61% for pure equity. Suggested ranges by life stage: in your 20s, 70 to 80% equity; 30s and 40s, 55 to 70%; 50s, 40 to 55%; and 60s and beyond, 25 to 40%, with the rest in debt plus 5 to 15% gold and cash. A shortcut is equity at about 100 minus your age. A pension supports more equity, while panicking at a 10% dip argues for less. All equity invites panic selling, and all FD or gold barely beats inflation after tax. And in a real panic, everything can dip briefly, gold included.", "The takeaway: The equity/debt/gold split sets how violent the ride is; stock picking only decides relative performance.", "Pause and answer: A 40% market fall hits a 70%-equity portfolio by roughly how much?", "⏸", "Answer: About 28%."]], ["diversification-without-overdoing-it", ["Diversification, without overdoing it. Diversification removes company risk, like fraud, debt or a lost client, but never market risk; in a crash, correlations jump towards 1 and most stocks fall together. Most of the benefit arrives by about 15 to 20 stocks. Beyond 20 to 30 names you add fees and confusion, not safety, which the course calls \"diworsification\", and forty overlapping funds just buy index returns at higher cost. A ₹5,000 a month SIP into a Nifty index fund owns 50 large companies at once. Meera put ₹5 lakh into one stock in 2018 and lost most of it when the story cracked. A checklist: no single stock above about 5 to 10% of the portfolio. At least 4 or 5 sectors, since ten bank stocks are one bet. Some non equity assets, debt or FDs and perhaps gold. And every holding nameable and tracked, in funds that don't secretly hold the same large caps.", "The takeaway: Diversification removes company risk, not market risk; most of the benefit arrives by 15–20 stocks.", "Pause and answer: What risk can diversification never remove?", "⏸", "Answer: Market risk: in a crash, correlations jump towards 1."]], ["sips-lumpsum-and-market-falls", ["SIPs, lumpsum and market falls. ₹10,000 a month for six months, as the NAV goes 100, 90, 70, 60, 75 and back to 100, buys 753.97 units for ₹60,000, an average cost of about ₹79.58. At ₹100 that's worth about ₹75,397: a gain, even though the price ended where it started. A ₹60,000 lumpsum at ₹100 would end flat. But a SIP's real value is behavioural: it keeps buying through fear. In a straight rising market, a lumpsum wins because the money works sooner, and a lumpsum suits money already in hand, a long horizon, and the stomach for an early fall. Stopping a SIP in a crash cancels the cheapest buying, as Ravi did in 2020 while Meera's ₹10,000 SIP kept going. Averaging fails on a single stock heading to zero, or when you must sell mid fall.", "The takeaway: A SIP's real value is behavioural, buying more units in falls; a lumpsum wins in a straight rising market.", "Pause and answer: Why did the SIP example show a gain when the price ended where it started?", "⏸", "Answer: It bought more units at lower prices, averaging about ₹79.58 against a final ₹100."]], ["rebalancing-sell-winners-buy-laggards", ["Rebalancing means selling winners and buying laggards. Start with ₹10 lakh split ₹6 lakh equity and ₹4 lakh debt, 60 to 40. Equity doubles, so you now have ₹12 lakh plus ₹4 lakh, ₹16 lakh at 75 to 25. The target, 60% of ₹16 lakh, is ₹9.6 lakh of equity and ₹6.4 lakh of debt, so you sell ₹2.4 lakh of equity and move it to debt. Three methods. Calendar: reset on a fixed date, such as yearly. Band: act when a sleeve drifts 5 to 10 points, so a 60% target uses a 50 to 70% band. And hybrid: check on the date, but trade only if a band is breached. Drift is a hidden risk increase, not a win. Once a year is enough for beginners, because rebalancing more often adds capital gains tax, exit loads and charges. You can also route fresh SIP money to the laggard to rebalance without selling. And rebalance only between sound, diversified sleeves, never into a collapsing stock.", "The takeaway: Drift is a hidden risk increase; rebalance on a calendar, a band, or both.", "Pause and answer: A 60/40 portfolio drifts to 75/25 at ₹16 lakh. What trade restores it?", "⏸", "Answer: Sell ₹2.4 lakh of equity and move it to debt (₹9.6 lakh / ₹6.4 lakh)."]], ["concentrated-stock-risk", ["Concentrated stock risk. An employee holding employer stock carries one bet three ways: salary, vested ESOPs or RSUs, and shares bought, and a bad patch can cut all three at once. Concentration builds fortunes and destroys them from the same starting decision, and trimming keeps upside while limiting ruin. The traps are employer loyalty, a family business plus same sector savings, a favourite stock, and IPO hype, and a healthy 10% position left alone can drift to a reckless 60%. The de risking steps: add up your true exposure, salary, vested and unvested ESOPs, shares and fund weight, as a share of net worth. Set a ceiling while calm, illustratively 10 to 20% of investable wealth in one stock. Trim on a schedule, a set fraction each quarter, using vesting dates and strong rallies. Plan tax timing, possibly across financial years, and redeploy into broad index funds or other sectors. And keep 3 to 6 months of expenses outside the employer's stock.", "The takeaway: Employer stock can be one bet three ways (salary, ESOPs, shares); add up true exposure and trim to limit ruin.", "Pause and answer: Why is employer stock riskier than it looks?", "⏸", "Answer: Salary, vested options and owned shares can all fall at once in a bad patch."]], ["investor-mistakes-that-look-smart", ["Investor mistakes that look smart, and better moves. Averaging down with no thesis: instead, have a written reason the business is sound, a fixed total size, and pre planned tranches. Chasing last year's winner: stick to the plan and diversify broadly. Panic selling a crash: own only what you can hold through a 30 to 40% fall, and keep the emergency fund. \"It fell, so it's cheap\": judge the business, not the old high, because a 70% fall can be followed by another 70%. And trusting a fat dividend yield: yield is dividend over price, so it spikes as the price collapses; check why. Meera bought at ₹500 and averaged down at ₹400, ₹300 and ₹200 without asking why it fell, turning a ₹50,000 mistake into ₹2 lakh. Each trap feels rational in the moment, so ask for the actual reason before every add, sale or buy.", "The takeaway: Averaging down without a thesis, chasing winners and trusting fat yields feel smart; judge the business, not the old high.", "Pause and answer: Why can a high dividend yield be a trap?", "⏸", "Answer: Yield = dividend ÷ price, so it spikes as the price collapses; check why the price fell."]]], "trader": [[null, ["Section four: trader risk management. This is where position sizing, stops and limits live."]], ["trading-is-not-investing", ["Trading is not investing. An investor owns a slice of a business; a trader owns a bet on a price move. What works for the investor is time, growth and compounding; for the trader, a repeatable edge. The investor holds for years, the trader for minutes to days. The investor decides the exit loosely; the trader decides it exactly, before entry. And the investor's biggest risk is the wrong business, while the trader's is skipping the risk rules. Every trade needs five tools: a pre defined exit, a position size, a stop loss, a journal entry, and a daily risk limit. Arjun bought at ₹1,020 because it \"looked strong\", held through ₹1,005 and ₹990 with no plan, and was down ₹18,000 by 3:15: investor habits on a trade. Edge is a small advantage that pays across many trades, not conviction in one. And relabelling a stuck trade as an investment isn't investing.", "The takeaway: A trade is a bet on a price move with an exit decided before entry; it needs exit, size, stop, journal and a daily limit.", "Pause and answer: Name the five tools every trade needs.", "⏸", "Answer: A pre-defined exit, a position size, a stop-loss, a journal entry and a daily risk limit."]], ["can-i-take-this-trade", ["This ladder decides whether you can take a trade your process calls for. First: has today already hit the daily limit of 3 losses or minus 3%? If yes, stop trading for today; losses already taken are sunk. If not: is the stop a real order, placed at entry and outside normal noise? If not, set a real stop order first, using structure or 1.5 to 2 times ATR, not a round number. If it is: counting correlated names as one bet, does your open heat stay under your cap? If not, pass, because the heat cap is reached, or close something first. If it does, take the trade at the computed size: quantity equals rupee risk divided by stop distance.", "The takeaway: Check the daily limit, a real stop order outside noise, and portfolio heat, then size = rupee risk ÷ stop distance.", "Pause and answer: Where should the stop sit?", "⏸", "Answer: As a real order placed at entry, beyond structure or 1.5–2× ATR, not on a round number."]], ["position-sizing-risk-sets-size", ["Position sizing: risk sets size. Four steps. One: choose the risk per trade, a small percentage of capital, 1% in the teaching example. Two: rupee risk is capital times that percentage, so ₹2 lakh times 1% is ₹2,000. Three: the stop distance is entry minus stop, ₹100 minus ₹92, ₹8 a share. Four: quantity is rupee risk divided by distance, ₹2,000 over ₹8, 250 shares, deploying ₹25,000. The table shows how the stop drives size: at 1% risk, a ₹4 stop allows 500 shares, an ₹8 stop 250, and a ₹16 stop 125. At 0.5% risk you halve those, and 2% doubles them, which is aggressive for beginners. The Python snippet is a small function doing exactly that: rupee risk, divided by the stop distance, rounded down to whole lots for F and O."]], ["position-sizing-risk-sets-size", ["The example calls give 250 shares for the ₹100 stock, and 65 units, one Nifty lot, for a Nifty trade at 24,000 with a 30 point stop. A wider stop means a smaller position for the same loss. Ravi bought 1,000 shares at ₹100 on a ₹2 lakh account by feel, and a fall to ₹85 cost ₹15,000, over 7%. Futures come in whole lots, so a Nifty lot of 65 with a 30 point stop risks ₹1,950, and option sellers face a tail far beyond the premium, so size tiny, often one lot or none. Percentage sizing shrinks after losses and grows after gains, while fixed rupee sizing doesn't adjust. And sizing caps the planned loss, not the worst case: a gap from a ₹92 stop to ₹80 doubles the hit to about ₹5,000.", "The takeaway: Fix rupee risk first; quantity = rupee risk ÷ stop distance, so the stop, not conviction, sets size.", "Pause and answer: ₹2 lakh capital, 1% risk, entry ₹100, stop ₹92. Quantity?", "⏸", "Answer: ₹2,000 ÷ ₹8 = 250 shares."]], ["stop-loss-five-kinds", ["Stop losses come in five kinds. A technical stop sits just beyond support, a swing low or a trendline, for when the idea depends on a level. A volatility stop sits 1.5 to 2 ATR from entry, for choppy instruments. A time stop exits after so many bars or days without progress, for when it should have moved by now. A mental stop lives only in your head, and should almost never be used. And a disaster stop is the maximum tolerable loss as a resting order, always, in every style. Place a real order at entry and move it only in your favour. Arjun's ₹95 \"plan\" drifted to ₹92, then ₹90 and lower, and he sold at ₹81. Avoid round numbers like ₹500 or Nifty 24,000, where stops crowd, and stops inside normal noise: a ₹3 stop on a stock that swings ₹8 a day is a donation. Stops fill at the next traded price, so a ₹95 stop on a stock opening at ₹80 fills near ₹80, and the defence is size. And stop outs before the move you expected are the insurance premium.", "The takeaway: Technical, volatility, time, mental and disaster stops; place a real order at entry and only move it in your favour.", "Pause and answer: Which stop type should every style have?", "⏸", "Answer: A disaster stop: the maximum tolerable loss as a resting order."]], ["win-rate-vs-payoff-expectancy", ["Win rate versus payoff: expectancy. Expectancy is the win rate times the average win, minus the loss rate times the average loss. The break even win rate is risk divided by risk plus reward: 25% at 1 to 3, 33% at 1 to 2, 50% at 1 to 1, and 83% if you risk 5 to make 1. Compare two traders risking ₹1,000 a trade. Trader A wins 80% of the time, but averages ₹500 wins and ₹2,500 losses: an expectancy of minus ₹100 a trade, minus ₹10,000 over 100 trades. Trader B wins only 40%, averaging ₹3,000 wins and ₹1,000 losses: plus ₹600 a trade, plus ₹60,000 over 100 trades. Chasing accuracy, banking small wins early and holding losers, turns a Trader B into a Trader A. Compute expectancy net of brokerage, STT, fees and slippage, and judge it over a few hundred trades, not 20. Typical active traders win 40 to 55%; option buyers win rarely but big; option sellers win often but face the fat loss.", "The takeaway: Expectancy, not win rate, decides profit; at 1:2 reward to risk you break even at a 33% win rate.", "Pause and answer: Trader A wins 80% with ₹500 wins and ₹2,500 losses. Expectancy?", "⏸", "Answer: 0.8 × 500 − 0.2 × 2,500 = −₹100 a trade."]], ["drawdowns-and-losing-streaks", ["Drawdowns and losing streaks. A 10% drawdown needs 11% to recover and feels annoying but normal. 20% needs 25%, and you feel rattled, checking often. 30% needs 43%. 40% needs 67%, and fear sets in: peak time for bad decisions. 50% needs 100%. At a 50% win rate, a run of 5 losses has odds of 1 in 32, and 6 losses 1 in 64, so over hundreds of trades, long streaks are certain. Even a 65% system can lose 7 of 20. Arjun took ₹2 lakh to ₹2.2 lakh, fell about 10% below that peak, then doubled his size twice, and dug a 45% hole in two weeks. Size down while you're down, never up. And SEBI's September 2024 study found the average loss making F and O trader lost about ₹1.2 lakh in FY24.", "The takeaway: Long losing streaks are certain over hundreds of trades; size down while down, never up.", "Pause and answer: At a 50% win rate, odds of five losses in a row?", "⏸", "Answer: 1 in 32."]], ["variance-or-a-broken-edge", ["This ladder separates variance from a broken edge. Inside a losing streak, halve the size first. Then ask: are losses staying at the planned size, with every rule followed? If not, it's a process problem: fix sizing and stops before anything else. If they are: is the drawdown within the backtest's deepest and longest? If not, the edge may be broken: pause the strategy, then review or retire it. If it is: are you judging over dozens of trades, not a handful? If not, it's too early to tell, so keep logging at reduced size. If you are, it's likely variance: keep the rules, and rebuild size slowly.", "The takeaway: In a streak, halve size; if rules were followed and the drawdown sits inside backtest bounds over dozens of trades, it's likely variance.", "Pause and answer: First action inside a losing streak?", "⏸", "Answer: Halve the size."]], ["daily-weekly-and-monthly-risk-limits", ["Daily, weekly and monthly risk limits, on a ₹1 lakh example. Per trade: about 1%, ₹1,000, built into the size. Per day: 3 losing trades or minus 3%, ₹3,000, whichever comes first, and then you're done for the day. Per week: about minus 6%, ₹6,000, and you're done until next week. Per strategy: its own limit over some weeks, after which you pause that method only. And per month: about minus 10%, ₹10,000, after which you stop real money, review, and practise in analyzer mode. Arjun lost ₹1,800 on two trades, sized up to win it back, took 9 trades instead of 3, and was down about 22% by lunch. A minus 3% daily stop would have ended that same morning nearly flat. For F and O, the course suggests minus 3% a day and minus 8 to 10% a month, and option sellers can exit a short at 2 to 3 times the premium collected. Limits that are too tight for the strategy's normal swing just cause noise stop outs. And the biggest failure is overriding one \"just this once\".", "The takeaway: Layered limits (1% a trade, −3% a day, −6% a week, −10% a month) stop one bad morning becoming a disaster.", "Pause and answer: What happens at the monthly limit?", "⏸", "Answer: Stop real money, review, and practise in analyzer mode."]], ["portfolio-heat-total-open-risk", ["Portfolio heat is your total open risk. Each position's risk is its stop distance times quantity, and heat is the sum across every open trade, as a percentage of capital. Gross exposure is longs plus shorts, and net is longs minus shorts. Priya, with ₹2 lakh, ran eight trades risking 1% each, all in financials: 8% heat. One bad sector day hit every stop for ₹16,000. Count themes, not tickers, because correlated names behave like one larger bet, and correlations rise towards 1 in stress. If you're long ₹60,000 and short ₹20,000, you have ₹80,000 gross in play and ₹40,000 net long. Example caps: about 5 to 6% total heat for active traders, 3 to 5% for F and O, no sector above about 20 to 25% for long term investors, and option sellers should cap their combined maximum loss and stress test a gap. The course cites lot sizes for the April to June 2026 cycle of 65 for the Nifty and 30 for Bank Nifty, and exchanges revise them.", "The takeaway: Heat is the sum of open position risk; correlated names count as one bet, especially in stress.", "Pause and answer: Priya ran eight 1% trades, all financials. What was her real risk?", "⏸", "Answer: 8% heat in one theme; one bad sector day hit every stop."]]], "leverage": [[null, ["Section five: leverage, margin and execution risk."]], ["leverage-measure-moves-against-your-margin", ["Leverage: measure moves against your margin. The position is margin times leverage, and the hit to your money is the price move times leverage, so a 2% move at 5 times is 10% of your capital. The adverse move that wipes out the whole margin: at 1 times, effectively never, though the stock itself can still go to zero; at 2 times, 50%; at 5 times, 20%; at 10 times, 10%; and at 20 times, just 5%. Vikram had a ₹50,000 account and a ₹2.5 lakh position, 5 times, and an ordinary 4% morning dip cost ₹10,000, 20% of the account. In India, intraday equity is about 5 times at most under peak margin rules. Futures carry built in leverage, which the course puts at about 5 to 6 times in one chapter and about 8 in another, depending on the margin assumed. Option buying is leveraged through the premium with a capped loss, while option selling posts margin and can lose far more than the premium. Higher leverage shortens the time you have to be right, because a normal dip forces you out before the move comes. The maximum offered is a ceiling, not a recommendation. Work backwards: pick the worst move you must survive, say 15%, and choose leverage that move can't wipe out, often 1 to 2 times. And a futures lot's leverage can only be cut by trading fewer lots.", "The takeaway: Hit to your money = price move × leverage; at 10× a 10% move erases the margin.", "Pause and answer: A 2% move at 5× leverage costs what share of your capital?", "⏸", "Answer: 10%."]], ["margin-calls-and-forced-exits", ["Margin calls and forced exits. Margin is a security deposit behind a leveraged position, not its price. Mark to market revalues the position at the latest price, with losses coming out of margin. A shortfall is remaining margin below what the position needs. Square off is the broker closing the position for you, without asking. And free margin is the buffer that absorbs normal swings. Forced exits fire when price has moved hardest against you, so they land later and lower than your own stop, and the difference is loss you never chose. Available margin is a ceiling, not capital: a trader with ₹1 lakh saw about ₹5 lakh \"available\", used nearly all of it on one index future, and was squared off near the intraday low. Peak margin rules collect margin upfront and check it at random intraday snapshots, and unclosed intraday positions are squared off mid afternoon at whatever price prevails; a sharp move can trigger it earlier. So size from your risk rule, then confirm margin stays comfortably free. Know how far price can move before a call, and keep your stop well before that point. And add funds or cut early, and exit intraday trades before the square off window.", "The takeaway: Forced exits fire when price is most against you, landing later and lower than your own stop; keep free margin as a buffer.", "Pause and answer: What is a square-off?", "⏸", "Answer: The broker closing your position for you, without asking, after a shortfall."]], ["the-broker-s-risk-system-rms", ["The broker's risk system, the RMS. It's an automatic margin guard that runs on the broker's rules and clock, protects the broker first, and closes positions at market on a shortfall without waiting for your stop. Cash versus collateral: F and O needs a minimum cash component, roughly half, as the course states for 2026, because daily losses settle in cash, which pledged shares can't pay. Positions funded entirely by collateral trigger cash shortfalls and penalties even when total margin looks full. Haircuts: pledging ₹1 lakh of stock typically gives about ₹80,000 to ₹90,000 of margin, with bigger haircuts for volatile or illiquid stocks, never zero. Auto square off: intraday positions close in a window around 3:20 to 3:30, depending on broker and segment, so exit before it yourself. Peak margin snapshots: a shortfall for a few minutes at a random snapshot can bring a penalty without any square off, so aim to be covered at every snapshot. Arjun ran pledged only intraday futures, lost track of time, and was closed at about 3:25 below his stop in a thin market, then drew a peak margin penalty on another day.", "The takeaway: The RMS protects the broker first; F&O needs a cash component because daily losses settle in cash, and pledged stock gets a haircut.", "Pause and answer: Why can an all-collateral F&O account hit a shortfall with 'full' margin?", "⏸", "Answer: Daily losses settle in cash, which pledged shares can't pay, so the minimum cash component is breached."]], ["slippage-liquidity-and-spread", ["Slippage, liquidity and spread. LTP is the last traded price, history rather than a price you can trade now. The best ask and bid are what you pay to buy now and receive to sell now. The spread is ask minus bid, paid on entry and again on exit. Impact cost is the drift in your average fill as a big order walks up the book. And slippage is expected price minus actual fill. Meera's far out of the money option showed ₹12. A market order for a few lots walked from ₹13 to 13.50 to 14, averaging about ₹13.40, so about 11% was lost before the trade even moved, with a similar slice due on exit. Market orders guarantee the fill, not the price; limit orders guarantee the price, not the fill. Default to limits at or near the touch, and use market orders only when it's urgent and the instrument is liquid. Index futures and large caps trade a few paise wide, while far out of the money options and small caps are thin, and your own order moves the price. A ₹2 scalp with ₹1.50 of round trip slippage keeps ₹0.50 even when right, before charges, while the same ₹1.50 barely matters on a ₹40 swing. Thin edges get eaten first.", "The takeaway: LTP is history; you pay the ask and receive the bid, and market orders walk the book in thin instruments.", "Pause and answer: What is slippage?", "⏸", "Answer: The difference between the expected price and the actual fill."]], ["gap-and-event-risk", ["Gap and event risk. Scheduled events have known dates and unknown outcomes: company results, RBI and US Fed decisions, the Union Budget on the 1st of February, weekly and monthly expiry, and election counting, as on the 4th of June 2024, with a sharp Nifty fall that morning. Unscheduled events come with no warning: demonetisation in November 2016, announced after the close; COVID in March 2020, with gap downs and lower circuit halts; wars; an overnight foreign bank collapse; and sudden regulatory orders. The key point: a stop caps the price you trigger at, not the price you fill at. Buy at ₹105 with a ₹100 stop; poor results come out, and it opens at ₹85. Planned risk was ₹5 a share; actual loss is ₹20. True overnight risk is the size of the surprise. On ₹1 lakh, a 15% gap costs ₹15,000 on a full ₹1 lakh position and ₹7,500 on half that: you choose only the size. Before an event, ask: can I absorb a 10% adverse open in rupees? Is there leverage I can close before the bell? Am I short an option with open ended loss? Protective options cap the loss but bleed premium, and going to cash avoids gaps but misses moves.", "The takeaway: A stop caps the trigger price, not the fill; scheduled and unscheduled events can gap straight through it.", "Pause and answer: Bought at ₹105, stop ₹100, stock opens at ₹85. Where does the stop fill?", "⏸", "Answer: Near ₹85: a stop limits the trigger, not the fill."]], ["hold-this-position-overnight", ["This ladder decides whether to hold an open position overnight. First: is a results, RBI, Budget, expiry or counting day event due before the next session? If yes, cut size before the close and drop the leverage. If not: is it a naked short option with open ended loss? If yes, hedge it or close it, because a protective option defines the worst case. If not: could you take a 10% adverse open in rupees and keep trading? If not, shrink until you could, because the gap, not the stop, sets the loss. If you could, carry it, but treat the stop as absent until the market reopens.", "The takeaway: Cut size before known events, hedge naked shorts, and carry only what survives a 10% adverse open.", "Pause and answer: A naked short option at the close: carry it?", "⏸", "Answer: Hedge it or close it; a protective option defines the worst case."]]], "fno": [[null, ["Section six: F and O risk for beginners."]], ["why-f-o-is-a-different-risk-world", ["Why F and O is a different risk world. Stocks have no expiry; F and O has fixed expiries, weekly for index options and monthly for futures. Stocks on delivery carry little leverage; F and O has it built in. A stock loss stays on paper until sold; F and O settles in cash, daily. Stocks have no time decay; options lose value every day. The minimum stock position is one share; in F and O it's one lot, about 65 for the Nifty and 30 for Bank Nifty. And while shorting cash is hard, an option seller can lose many times the premium. Arjun bought 5 lots of the Nifty 24,500 call at ₹40, 325 units, ₹13,000. The Nifty rallied to about 24,300 and expired at 24,350, below the strike: right direction, total loss. \"It's only ₹40\" isn't low risk. SEBI's September 2024 study found about 91% of individual F and O traders lost money in FY24, the average loser about ₹1.2 lakh. Approach with humility and tiny size.", "The takeaway: F&O adds expiry, built-in leverage, daily cash settlement, time decay and lot sizes; right direction can still mean total loss.", "Pause and answer: How did Arjun lose everything while right on direction?", "⏸", "Answer: NIFTY rose but expired below his 24,500 strike, so the calls expired worthless."]], ["futures-linear-no-cushion-billed-daily", ["Futures are linear, have no cushion, and bill you daily. At about ₹65 a point per Nifty lot: 10 points is ₹650, a normal wiggle; 50 points is ₹3,250, one headline; 100 points is ₹6,500, a bad session; 200 points is ₹13,000, a two day move; and 500 points is ₹32,500, a gap or shock day. One lot at 24,000 is about ₹15.6 lakh of exposure, against margin of about ₹1.8 to 2 lakh, roughly 8 times leverage. Now the daily bill. On a ₹2 lakh account, moves of minus 40, 50, 60 and 30 points take the balance from ₹1,97,400 to ₹1,94,150, then ₹1,90,250, then ₹1,88,300. Then margin rises, and the position is squared off. Overnight gaps hit in full: 300 points on one lot is ₹19,500, debited the same day. Monthly contracts must be rolled, paying spread, brokerage and slippage each time, and the basis, future minus spot, converges to zero at expiry. Exchanges raise margins in volatile markets, often just as MTM losses arrive. So don't \"hold and wait\" a future like a stock: fund a bad week in cash, or don't carry it.", "The takeaway: One NIFTY lot moves about ₹65 a point with no cushion; losses are debited daily and gaps hit in full.", "Pause and answer: A 300-point gap on one NIFTY lot costs about how much?", "⏸", "Answer: About ₹19,500, debited the same day."]], ["option-buyer-capped-loss-low-odds", ["The option buyer: a capped loss, but low odds. Maximum loss is the premium, with no margin calls, and a call's breakeven is strike plus premium, reached before expiry. Three killers. Killer one, time decay: time value leaks daily, steepest in the last 2 or 3 days, and cheap out of the money weeklies are almost all time value. Killer two, timing: the move comes after expiry, or is too small or too slow. Killer three, IV crush: events inflate premiums, then implied volatility collapses, so an option worth ₹90 before results was ₹55 after, even though the price rose a little. Arjun bought far out of the money weekly calls at ₹6 most Tuesdays, the NSE weekly expiry, ₹390 a lot, and had 4 wins and 9 losses in three months: a slow bleed. Before buying: know the rupee premium at risk and accept losing all of it. Prefer near the money strikes with real value and more than 1 or 2 days left. Avoid IV inflated by an event, and set a price or time exit before buying.", "The takeaway: Max loss is the premium, but time decay, timing and IV crush make most cheap OTM buys lose.", "Pause and answer: Name the three killers of option buying.", "⏸", "Answer: Time decay, timing (the move comes late or small), and IV crush."]], ["option-seller-high-probability-not-low-risk", ["The option seller: high probability is not low risk. Nine wins of ₹2,000 make ₹18,000, and one loss of ₹40,000 wipes it out. Expected value is 0.9 times 2,000 minus 0.1 times 40,000, minus ₹2,200 a trade, so 10 trades lose ₹22,000 at a 90% win rate. Comparing the two sides: the buyer pays the premium, the seller collects it. The buyer's best case is large and open ended, the seller's small and capped. The buyer's worst case is the premium, the seller's large or unlimited. And the buyer needs no margin beyond the premium, while the seller posts large margin that can rise. The seller's equity curve is a gentle staircase, then a cliff, and a volatility spike alone can reprice an ₹80 option to ₹500. Index options are cash settled, while stock options and futures can go to physical delivery, so if you don't understand settlement, close before expiry. The beginner default is defined risk: sell one option and buy a cheaper, further out one, a spread, and keep size constant through winning streaks. Never sell naked.", "The takeaway: A 90% win rate can still lose money when one loss is 20 times a win; the equity curve is a staircase then a cliff.", "Pause and answer: Nine wins of ₹2,000 and one loss of ₹40,000: expected value per trade?", "⏸", "Answer: 0.9 × 2,000 − 0.1 × 40,000 = −₹2,200."]], ["greeks-as-a-risk-dashboard", ["The Greeks as a risk dashboard. Delta measures the price change per 1 point move, so about 0.5 means about 50 paise, and it hurts anyone on the wrong side. Theta is daily time decay, weekends included, which hurts buyers every day and is income for sellers. Vega is sensitivity to implied volatility, hurting buyers after an IV crush and sellers on IV spikes. And gamma is how fast delta changes, explosive near the strike at expiry, hurting holders near the strike on expiry day. Arjun bought a Nifty call at ₹120 before an RBI policy. The Nifty rose about 80 points by the next morning, and the call was worth ₹95, sunk by theta and vega. Read all four gauges, not just delta. Greeks describe small, smooth moves, and gaps break them. And they tell you which risks you carry, not how much, so sizing is a separate job.", "The takeaway: Delta is direction, theta is daily decay, vega is volatility, gamma is how fast delta changes, explosive near the strike at expiry.", "Pause and answer: Which Greek hurts buyers every day, weekends included?", "⏸", "Answer: Theta."]], ["expiry-day-risk", ["Expiry day risk. Weekly index options settle on Tuesday at NSE and Thursday at BSE, as the course states, and exchanges revise these. Gamma peaks near the strike, so a 20 to 30 point wiggle can swing the premium within minutes. The liquidity trap: a ₹2 screen price may sell for 50 paise, or find no bid at all, so assume far out of the money expiry options at 50 paise to ₹1 are worthless. Pin risk: the index sits on a heavy strike, then breaks away. Execution freeze: queues lengthen and fills land far from the screen. And adjustment panic: adding to, hedging or rolling a losing expiry trade usually deepens it. Arjun put ₹8,000 into a ₹4 call hoping for a ten bagger. The Nifty rose about 30 points, the call touched ₹5, was 30 paise at 3:20, and zero at the close. Before any expiry trade, ask: is it cheap because it's unlikely? Is there a real bid to exit into? Is the loss limit set, and will you take it without adjusting?", "The takeaway: On expiry, gamma, illiquidity, pin risk and execution freezes make small moves violent; assume far-OTM options are worthless.", "Pause and answer: On which weekday do NSE weekly index options settle?", "⏸", "Answer: Tuesday (Thursday at BSE, as the course states)."]]], "last": [[null, ["Final section: staying current, and quick reference."]], ["keep-the-method-refresh-the-numbers", ["Keep the method, refresh the numbers. Lot sizes and contract specs come from NSE's equity derivatives pages. The expiry calendar and holidays come from the NSE and BSE websites. F and O profit and loss studies come from SEBI reports and press releases. Margin, peak margin, T plus 1 and pledge rules come from SEBI and NSE circulars, and your broker. And charges, STT, fees, GST and stamp duty, come from notifications and your contract note. Date stamp every figure, for example \"Nifty lot 65, checked 26 June 2026\", because an undated number looks equally trustworthy at 12 months and 12 minutes. Meera planned about ₹1,000 of risk a lot using an old 50 unit lot; at 65 units, the same ₹20 a unit loss is ₹1,300, about 30% more risk than intended. Your broker may block more margin or cut off earlier than the headline rule, and its figures govern your account, so re check just before placing the order.", "The takeaway: Date-stamp every figure; lot sizes, margins and charges change, so check the source before trading.", "Pause and answer: Where do you check current margin and peak-margin rules?", "⏸", "Answer: SEBI and NSE circulars, and your broker."]], ["numbers-at-a-glance", ["The numbers at a glance. Recovery gain is the loss over one minus the loss, so minus 20% needs plus 25%, and minus 50% needs plus 100%. Quantity is capital times risk percentage, divided by entry minus stop. Expectancy is win rate times average win, minus loss rate times average loss. The break even win rate is risk over risk plus reward. Heat is the sum of stop distance times quantity, counting correlated names as one. The leverage wipe out move is 100% divided by leverage, so 5 times means a 20% move. Example limits are 1% a trade, minus 3% a day, minus 6% a week, and minus 10% a month. And the emergency fund is 3 to 6 months of expenses, or 9 to 12 if income is lumpy.", "The takeaway: Recovery gain = loss ÷ (1 − loss); quantity = capital × risk% ÷ stop distance; wipe-out move = 100% ÷ leverage.", "Pause and answer: Formula for the break-even win rate?", "⏸", "Answer: Risk ÷ (risk + reward)."]], ["before-every-trade", ["Before every trade. The money comes from the right bucket: the base is full, there's no costly debt, and it isn't goal money. Rupee risk is fixed first, and quantity is computed from a real stop. Daily, weekly and monthly limits aren't hit, and heat is under the cap. Leverage is chosen so the worst plausible move doesn't wipe the margin. The event calendar is checked, and overnight size survives a gap. The instrument is liquid, you're using a limit order, and costs are counted against the target. And for options, the Greeks are read, IV is checked, and the worst case is defined.", "The takeaway: Right bucket, rupee risk fixed, limits and heat clear, leverage survivable, events checked, liquid instrument, worst case defined.", "Pause and answer: What must be fixed before quantity?", "⏸", "Answer: The rupee risk; then quantity comes from a real stop."]], ["about-this-sheet", ["This sheet condenses the free Risk Management course from OpenAlgo Open Varsity, 33 chapters in 7 modules, into reference cards in our own words. Lot sizes, margins, settlement days and SEBI figures are as the course gives them in 2026 and will change, and every percentage and rupee amount is a teaching illustration. Each section links back to its chapters. It's educational only, not investment advice. That's the sheet. Survive first, and thanks for listening."]], ["numbers-that-expire", ["The numbers that expire card collects every figure on this sheet that a regulator, exchange or budget can change, like lot sizes, tax rates, STT and expiry days. Each one shows its current value and when it last changed, with a link to the source. Use it as a checklist: before you trade on any number from this sheet, check it there, and then check the source, because these move."]]]};
